---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageAttribution from '../../components/PageAttribution.astro';

const title = "The 64 Hexagrams, Listed - AstrologyLib";
const description = "All 64 I Ching hexagrams in order, with plain-English keywords: from Qian (1) and Kun (2) to Ji Ji (63) and Wei Ji (64).";

const faqSchema = {
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How many hexagrams are in the I Ching?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Sixty-four. Each hexagram has six lines, and the 64 are every possible combination of the eight three-line trigrams stacked in pairs."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why does the I Ching end with Wei Ji (not yet across)?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The final hexagram, Wei Ji (64, 未濟), means the crossing is not complete. Ending on incompletion is deliberate: no situation is ever final, and every ending starts a new cycle."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What are the upper and lower trigrams?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Each hexagram is two trigrams stacked: the lower (inner) trigram describes the inner situation, the upper (outer) trigram the outer situation. Reading which trigram sits where is the first step of structural interpretation."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do I need to memorize all 64 hexagrams?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. Practitioners memorize the structure (trigrams, moving lines) and look up individual hexagrams. This list exists so you can find any hexagram by number in seconds."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Where do the hexagram names come from?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The names and judgments come from the Zhou Yi core text of the Western Zhou period. Later commentaries, especially the Ten Wings, expanded their philosophical meaning."
      }
    }
  ]
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} active="learn" schemaJson={faqSchema}>
  <div class="article-head">
    <div class="crumb"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/learn/">Learn</a> › The 64 Hexagrams, Listed</div>
    <h1><span class="zh">六十四卦</span>The 64 Hexagrams, Listed</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="layout">
    <aside class="toc">
      <div class="t-label">In this lesson</div>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#how">How the list is ordered</a></li>
        <li><a href="#table">All 64 hexagrams</a></li>
        <li><a href="#use">How to use this list</a></li>
        <li><a href="#where">Where this leads</a></li>
      </ul>
    </aside>

    <article class="article-body">
      <PageAttribution
        sources={[
          { label: 'Zhou Yi (周易) · King Wen sequence', url: '/glossary/' },
          { label: 'Hexagram judgments and line texts', url: '/tools/hexagram/' }
        ]}
        verified="2026-10-08"
        version="1.0.0"
      />

      <p class="lead">All sixty-four hexagrams in their traditional King Wen sequence, each with a one-line keyword. Find your hexagram's number, read the keyword, then study its judgment in full. The book famously ends not with completion but with Wei Ji - not yet across - a deliberate reminder that no situation is ever final.</p>

      <div class="pdf-box" style="border:1px solid var(--ink-200);border-radius:10px;padding:18px 20px;margin:24px 0;background:var(--paper-2,#faf8f2)">
        <p style="margin:0 0 10px"><strong>Free one-page PDF.</strong> 64 Hexagrams One-Page: Numbers, Names & Keywords — print it, keep it. No signup needed.</p>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/pdf/i-ching-hexagrams-cheatsheet.pdf" download>Download free PDF</a>
      </div>

      <h2 id="how">How the list is ordered</h2>
      <p>This is the received <strong>King Wen sequence</strong> (文王卦序), the order the hexagrams have appeared in for over two thousand years. It is not ranked by importance: the first two establish the poles (pure yang, pure yin), pairs like Zhun/Meng (3/4) show difficulty at the start, and the final pair Ji Ji/Wei Ji (63/64) closes the book on "already across / not yet across."</p>

